mmap:
void *mmap(
    void *addr,
    size_t length,
    int prot,
    int flags,
    int fd,
    off_t offset
);


addr: send virtual address you want, with flags like:
        MAP_FIXED(16): kernal will allocate that range for your map, overwrite if ave to
        MAP_FIXED_NOREPLACE: if address not available, dont overwrite, fail
        MAP_ANONYMOUS: give any virtual address
        : pass in NULL/0 to ask for any mapping with MAP_ANONYMOUS flag

lenght: len of virtual page mapping you want
        : make sure it's in page size 4096(linux)
        : else it returns error
        IMPORTANT : practically ran fine, no error on non page_size_multiple length

prot: what can cpu do with this mapping?
        PROT_NONE(0) : cannot do naything
        PROT_READ(1) : can only read from this mapping
        PROT_WRITE(2) : can only writ efrom this mapping
        PROT_EXEC(4) : can execute from this mapping

flags: what kind of mapping is this?   
        MAP_PRIVATE(2) : mapping to this process are private, no other process can see the mapping this process is using

        MAP_SHARED(1) : mapping from this process can be seen and modified by other process

        MAP_ANONYMOUS(32) : mapping is not backed by a regular file(doesn't matter what fd you give, it will map random virtual address in mmap regiion)
        : this flag is not used alone

        MAP_FIXED
        MAP_POPULATE
        MAP_STACK

fd: fd of the object
        -1 fd : use this with the MAP_ANONYMOUS flag, tells kernal this is not a regular fd, give random memory mappings
        : if you close fd after mapping it, it does not remove the mapping. Therefore, you can still access that object

offset: offset from beginning from where the mapping should begin,
        offset 100: mapping[0] file[500]

mmap returns the starting virtual address of the mapping.

