structure: That the kernal reads from to know what to do on a specific sigaction

align 8                 ; not necessary but tells assembler to move the sa to a address 
                        ; thats divisible by 8
sa: 
   dq handler           ; for the address of handler (which is a funtion defined already)
   dq 0                 ; for the flags
   dq 0                 ; for the restorer
   times 16 dq 0        ; 16 times dq = 16x8 = 128bytes for mask

                     sa
                      ↓
      ┌──────────────────────────────┐
+0    │ address of handler           │  8 bytes
      ├──────────────────────────────┤
+8    │ flags = 0                    │  8 bytes
      ├──────────────────────────────┤
+16   │ restorer = 0                 │  8 bytes
      ├──────────────────────────────┤
+24   │                              │
      │                              │
      │       signal mask            │  128 bytes
      │       all zero               │
      │                              │
      └──────────────────────────────┘


      restorer: The sys_rt_sigreturn syscall needs to be performed after handler
                Normal ret wont do. So historucally you can give kernal the address 
                where restorer function lives. All the function does is executes a piece of code after handler does a normal ret. 
        case1:  if i make handler and restorer as seperate function,
                        handler executes normal ret, kernal executes the restorer function which ultimately does the sys_rt_sigreturn syscall.
        case2:  i can write the sys_rt_sigreturn syscall inside the handler, no need to 
                        have a seperate restorer function.
                i will also have to give flag : SA_RESTORER equ 0x04000000
                in the flag section to tell kernal i have specified a restorer

        [IMPORTANT]: X86-64 only support CASE 1.



The actual syscall:

    mov rax, 13         ; sys_rt_sigreturn
    mov rdi, 2          ; SIGINT
    mov rsi, sa         ; pointer to the struct
    xor rdx, rdx        ; oldact = NULL, address to a space where you want old config
    mov r10, 8          ; sizeof(sigset_t) in x84_64 is 8byts,This is what kernal expects.
    syscall

    oldact : If you want kernel, give me the previous signal configuration here.
             But i don't care about the old signal config
        Q . how do i know the size to reserve at the address that i am giving kernal?
        Ans 
                section .bss
                old_sa:
                    resb SIGACTION_SIZE
        Yes SIGACTION_SIZE is the size defined in ABI.
